# Frontend Accessibility Rules

## Related Rules
- @frontend-rules.mdc for general frontend guidelines
- @frontend/core/performance.mdc for accessibility performance
- @frontend/core/testing.mdc for accessibility testing

## Overview
This document defines the rules and best practices for ensuring web accessibility (a11y) in Ruby on Rails applications.

## Accessibility Principles

### 1. Semantic HTML with Rails Helpers
- Use proper Rails form helpers
- Implement proper heading hierarchy
- Use semantic Rails view helpers
- Ensure proper document structure
- Use proper label associations

### 2. ARIA Implementation
- Use proper Rails UJS attributes
- Implement proper roles
- Use ARIA live regions for dynamic content
- Ensure proper ARIA states
- Maintain ARIA relationships

### 3. Keyboard Navigation
- Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible
- Implement proper focus management
- Maintain logical tab order
- Provide visible focus indicators
- Handle keyboard shortcuts

## Implementation Guidelines

### Rails Form Helpers
```ruby
# Good
<%= form_with(model: @user, local: true) do |f| %>
  <%= f.label :email, "Email Address" %>
  <%= f.email_field :email, aria: { describedby: "email-help" } %>
  <span id="email-help">Enter your email address</span>
<% end %>

# Bad
<%= form_with(model: @user, local: true) do |f| %>
  <div>Email:</div>
  <%= f.email_field :email %>
<% end %>
```

### Rails UJS Implementation
```ruby
# Good
<%= link_to "Update", update_path, 
    class: "btn",
    data: { 
      remote: true,
      method: :patch,
      confirm: "Are you sure?",
      disable_with: "Updating..."
    },
    aria: { label: "Update user profile" } %>

# Bad
<%= link_to "Update", update_path, 
    class: "btn",
    onclick: "updateEverything()" %>
```

## Color and Contrast
- Maintain minimum contrast ratios
- Don't rely solely on color for information
- Provide alternative text for images
- Ensure text is readable
- Test with color blindness simulators

## Screen Reader Compatibility
- Provide descriptive alt text
- Use proper ARIA labels
- Implement skip links
- Ensure proper reading order
- Test with screen readers

## Testing Requirements
- Automated accessibility testing
- Manual keyboard testing
- Screen reader testing
- Color contrast testing
- Focus management testing

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Not using Rails form helpers
- Missing alt text
- Poor color contrast
- Inaccessible forms
- Missing focus indicators

## Validation Checklist
- [ ] Semantic HTML implemented
- [ ] ARIA attributes used correctly
- [ ] Keyboard navigation works
- [ ] Color contrast meets standards
- [ ] Screen reader compatibility verified
- [ ] Forms are accessible
- [ ] Focus management implemented
- [ ] Skip links provided
